{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/a2-modern-australia-part-2/audio/main-b706c28cf337.mp3","durationSec":223.752,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":2.22,"text":"Canberra has a lake in the centre of the city."},{"start":3.14,"end":7.26,"text":"Lake Burley Griffin looks like part of the natural landscape, but people made it."},{"start":7.9,"end":11.54,"text":"The lake was a central part of Walter Burley Griffin's plan for Canberra."},{"start":12.3,"end":17.28,"text":"Today, parks around the water have picnic areas, walking tracks and cycling paths."},{"start":18.06,"end":21.3,"text":"People can also row, sail, canoe and kayak on the lake."},{"start":21.9,"end":23.58,"text":"There are clubs for these activities."},{"start":24.74,"end":29.86,"text":"Tourism Australia gives weekend bike rides around the lake as one example of life in Canberra."},{"start":30.58,"end":32.72,"text":"This helps explain an outdoor routine."},{"start":33.8,"end":37.9,"text":"Enjoying the outdoors needs more than good weather: people need somewhere to go."},{"start":38.66,"end":42.96,"text":"Here, the city's design gives them places to walk, ride or meet friends."},{"start":43.92,"end":48.62,"text":"A weekend ride is a personal choice, but the path is something the city provides."},{"start":49.58,"end":53.9,"text":"A familiar drink can tell us something about the people who helped shape a city."},{"start":54.32,"end":59.44,"text":"After the Second World War, Greek and Italian migrants settled in Melbourne and Sydney."},{"start":60.32,"end":64.82,"text":"Historian Garritt Van Dyk connects Australia's coffee culture with these communities."},{"start":65.86,"end":70.2,"text":"Italian communities in regional Queensland also helped shape coffee habits."},{"start":71.08,"end":73.62,"text":"The useful point is how a custom travels."},{"start":74.5,"end":78,"text":"People move to a new country and bring familiar habits with them."},{"start":78.58,"end":83,"text":"Over time, those habits can become part of other people's daily lives too."},{"start":83.72,"end":86.64,"text":"Something that once arrived from elsewhere starts to feel local."},{"start":87.62,"end":91.74,"text":"Today, Australian cafés and restaurants serve food from many countries."},{"start":92.72,"end":96.24,"text":"When you order a coffee, you may simply want a drink or a break with a friend."},{"start":97.26,"end":102.12,"text":"Behind that ordinary moment is a longer story of people moving and sharing ways of life."},{"start":103,"end":106.16,"text":"At a sausage sizzle, people sell cooked sausages in bread."},{"start":106.64,"end":111.74,"text":"Outside some Australian shops, these simple food sales help local groups raise money."},{"start":112.58,"end":120.34,"text":"For example, Canberra Transport Museum advertised a fundraising sausage sizzle at Bunnings Warehouse Belconnen."},{"start":120.34,"end":127.78,"text":"Its notice invited people to buy food to help pay the museum's ongoing costs: the everyday bills involved in keeping it running."},{"start":128.48,"end":131.2,"text":"Bunnings gives another example from Gympie."},{"start":131.2,"end":136.58,"text":"Sausage-sizzle fundraisers for a Riding for the Disabled Centre helped pay for fences and gardens."},{"start":137.66,"end":141.32,"text":"Here, selling food supported a practical local project."},{"start":142.12,"end":143.78,"text":"The purpose of the purchase matters."},{"start":144.52,"end":148.1,"text":"You get something to eat, and the sale also supports a group."},{"start":148.1,"end":151.48,"text":"Compare that with friends sharing a barbecue in a park."},{"start":152.34,"end":155.82,"text":"The food may be similar, while the reason for gathering is different."},{"start":156.42,"end":161.22,"text":"Looking at who organises an event and where the money goes tells you more than the food alone."},{"start":162,"end":164.5,"text":"A phone can bring work into an evening at home."},{"start":165.62,"end":168.24,"text":"Australia has a rule called the right to disconnect."},{"start":169.34,"end":176.76,"text":"Employees can refuse to check, read or answer work contact outside their working hours, unless refusing is unreasonable."},{"start":177.98,"end":179.82,"text":"There is an important distinction here."},{"start":180.48,"end":182.8,"text":"The rule does not ban sending a late message."},{"start":183.48,"end":186.66,"text":"It concerns whether the worker can reasonably leave it unanswered."},{"start":187.46,"end":191.46,"text":"The reason for the contact matters, along with how much it disturbs the worker."},{"start":191.98,"end":197.32,"text":"The person's job, personal circumstances and extra pay for being available also matter."},{"start":198.12,"end":201.7,"text":"Someone working overtime or on call may still need to respond."},{"start":202.58,"end":204.82,"text":"Being on call means being available when needed."},{"start":205.58,"end":209.4,"text":"The rule shows that protecting free time involves practical choices."},{"start":209.94,"end":214.32,"text":"A message can wait in some situations and need attention in others."},{"start":215.32,"end":223.12,"text":"Employers and employees are encouraged to discuss what they expect, so people understand their responsibilities before the phone rings."}],"words":[{"text":"Canberra","start":0,"end":0.38},{"text":"has","start":0.38,"end":0.66},{"text":"a","start":0.66,"end":0.84},{"text":"lake","start":0.84,"end":1.06},{"text":"in","start":1.06,"end":1.3},{"text":"the","start":1.3,"end":1.4},{"text":"centre","start":1.4,"end":1.6},{"text":"of","start":1.6,"end":1.84},{"text":"the","start":1.84,"end":1.98},{"text":"city","start":1.98,"end":2.22},{"text":"Lake","start":3.14,"end":3.18},{"text":"Burley","start":3.18,"end":3.56},{"text":"Griffin","start":3.56,"end":3.84},{"text":"looks","start":3.84,"end":4.14},{"text":"like","start":4.14,"end":4.4},{"text":"part","start":4.4,"end":4.62},{"text":"of","start":4.62,"end":4.76},{